    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       },
       {
        "type": "highlight",
        "attrs": {
         "hex": "FF0000"
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "Steady, boring upper abd pain"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " (typically epigastric or LUQ), typically severe enough to need parenteral opioids, and it can radiate thru to the back; USU sudden in gallstone pancreatitis, over a few days in alcoholic pancreatitis; sitting up and leaning forward may reduce it; N/V are common"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Pts tend to appear acutely ill and sweaty; tachy, tachypnea and hypotension can occur in mod and severe cases; over 60% develop a fever"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
